<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#golden-urn">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Golden Urn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1793">1793</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Golden Urn was a method introduced by the Qing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a> in 1793 for choosing high Tibetan and Mongolian reincarnate lamas, including the Dalai and Panchen Lamas. The names of candidates were written on slips placed in an urn and one was drawn by lot under the supervision of the Qing ambans. Intended to curb manipulation by noble families, it was used in some recognitions and set aside in others.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The urn grew out of the settlement that followed the Qing victory in the second invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-nepalese-war">Sino-Nepalese War</a>. Its rules formed Article One of the 29-Article Ordinance for the More Effective Governing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, which covered the selection of rinpoches, lamas and other high offices of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>. In The Discourse of Lama, published in 1792, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a> set out the history of lamas and of the reincarnation system, argued that the system was man-made and laid down rules to remove its drawbacks.</p><p>The ordinance let the Qing emperors control the selection, so that Mongol and Tibetan nobles could not use it to seize religious power and lamas could not combine with secular forces. Presented as a means of preventing cheating and corruption, it also placed the emperor as a religious authority able to put forward candidates.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The emperor issued two urns. One, enshrined in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokhang">Jokhang Temple</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>, served for the Dalai and Panchen Lamas; the other, in the Yonghe Temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, for the Mongolian Jebtsundamba Khutughtu. The ritual was written by the 8th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>, Jamphel Gyatso. Each candidate's name and date of birth were written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a>, Han and Tibetan on metal or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> slips placed in the urn, and after prayers before the statue of the Jowo one slip was drawn.</p><p>The 7th Panchen Lama, Palden Tenpai Nyima, used the urn for the first time in 1822 to choose the 10th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>, Tsultrim Gyatso, and the 8th and 9th Panchen Lamas were also confirmed by it. Where it was not used, the amban was consulted, and Lhamo Dhondup was exempted from it when he became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/14th-dalai-lama">14th Dalai Lama</a> in 1940.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Later Chinese governments kept the method in law. In 1936 the Mongolian and Tibetan Affairs Commission of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> issued the Method of Reincarnation of Lamas, which required the death of a Dalai or Panchen Lama to be reported, candidates to be checked by the commission and a lot-drawing with the urn to be held. In 2007 the State Religious Affairs Bureau Order No. 5 of the People's Republic declared the urn applicable to lamas whose line of reincarnation went back in history.</p><p>The urn stood at the centre of the dispute of 1995. On 14 May the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a> announced from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> that Gedhun Choekyi Nyima was the reincarnation of the 10th Panchen Lama, before any drawing had taken place or the central government had made an announcement. The Chinese government objected; Chadrel Rinpoche was arrested that month and in 1997 sentenced to six years in prison on charges of splitting the country and leaking state secrets.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Golden Urn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…January 1940 the Regent Reting Rinpoche asked the Chinese central government to exempt him from the lot-drawing with the <b>Golden Urn</b>, which it approved, and after a ransom of 400,000 silver dragons was paid to Ma Bufang he travelled to Lhasa and was enthroned at the Potala Palace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet (1912-1951) · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Tibet’s reliance on reincarnation meant long regencies while each new Dalai Lama grew up, and outside powers manipulated the choice, as when the Qing introduced the <b>Golden Urn</b> lottery in 1793.</blockquote><a href="/government/theocracy">Theocracy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>After intervening against the Dzungars in 1720, the Qing Empire stationed ambans in Lhasa and introduced the <b>Golden Urn</b> in 1793, though their real authority fluctuated.</blockquote><a href="/state/ganden-phodrang">Ganden Phodrang</a></li></ul></section>
